"""Printed 273–276: source tangent/normal proofs and off-curve examples."""
from math12_reviewed_unit1 import heading,paragraph,page
from math12_reviewed_higher_derivatives import eq,note
PAGES=[page('141_page_1.jpg',273,[paragraph('The equation, being a quadratic in x, gives two values x₁,x₂ to be used in the line to obtain y₁,y₂. The set {(x₁,y₁),(x₂,y₂)} is the set of intersections. These points are real, coincident or imaginary according to the quadratic roots or its discriminant:'),eq(r'\mathrm{Disc}=4a^4m^2c^2+4(b^2-a^2m^2)(a^2)(c^2+b^2)>0,\quad\mathrm{real\ and\ different}'),eq(r'\mathrm{Disc}=4a^4m^2c^2+4(b^2-a^2m^2)(a^2)(c^2+b^2)=0,\quad\mathrm{real\ and\ coincident}'),eq(r'\mathrm{Disc}=4a^4m^2c^2+4(b^2-a^2m^2)(a^2)(c^2+b^2)<0,\quad\mathrm{imaginary}'),heading('Example 24'),paragraph('Find intersections of x−y−1=0 and hyperbola 4x²−y²=4.'),heading('Solution'),eq(r'x-y-1=0\quad\Rightarrow\quad y=x-1'),eq(r'4x^2-y^2=4','85'),paragraph('Use the line in the hyperbola to obtain x-coordinates:'),eq(r'4x^2-(x-1)^2=4'),eq(r'4x^2-(x^2+1-2x)-4=0\quad\Rightarrow\quad3x^2+2x-5=0\quad\Rightarrow\quad x=1,-\frac53'),paragraph('Use these x-coordinates in the line for y-coordinates:'),eq(r'x=1,-\frac53\quad\Rightarrow\quad y=0,-\frac83'),paragraph('Thus the two intersections (1,0),(−5/3,−8/3) are real and distinct, and the line intersects the hyperbola at those points.'),heading('(viii) Equation of a tangent line in slope-form'),paragraph('If m is the slope of the tangent to x²/a²−y²/b²=1, its equation has form y=mx+c. Here c is to be calculated from the tangency. Use the line in the hyperbola:'),eq(r'\frac{x^2}{a^2}-\frac{(mx+c)^2}{b^2}=1'),eq(r'x^2(b^2-a^2m^2)-2a^2mcx+a^2(c^2+b^2)=0'),note('The source again prints the wrong positive constant in the expanded quadratic, while its displayed discriminant correctly corresponds to the negative constant. Use (b²−a²m²)x²−2a²mcx−a²(c²+b²)=0. The discriminant is 4a²b²(c²+b²−a²m²) and the quadratic classification requires b²−a²m²≠0. Several source cross-reference/equation labels on this page are too faint to certify; the visible expressions and Example 24 values are retained.')]),
page('141_page_2.jpg',274,[paragraph('If the line touches the hyperbola, the quadratic equation has coincident roots and its discriminant equals zero:'),eq(r'4a^4m^2c^2+4(b^2-a^2m^2)(a^2)(c^2+b^2)=0'),eq(r'a^2m^2c^2+(b^2-a^2m^2)(c^2+b^2)=0'),paragraph('Divide out by 4a².'),eq(r'a^2m^2c^2-a^2m^2c^2-a^2m^2b^2+b^2c^2+b^4=0'),eq(r'-a^2m^2b^2+b^2c^2+b^4=0'),eq(r'-a^2m^2+c^2+b^2=0'),eq(r'c^2=a^2m^2-b^2\quad\Rightarrow\quad c=\pm\sqrt{a^2m^2-b^2}'),paragraph('This is the condition of tangency. Substitute c in the line to obtain the required tangent:'),eq(r'y=mx+c=mx\pm\sqrt{a^2m^2-b^2}'),heading('Remember'),paragraph('The equation of any tangent to hyperbola x²/a²−y²/b²=1 in slope-form is:'),eq(r'y=mx\pm\sqrt{a^2m^2+b^2}'),paragraph('The line y=mx+c touches x²/a²−y²/b²=1 under condition:'),eq(r'c=\pm\sqrt{a^2m^2-b^2}'),heading('Example 25'),paragraph('For what value of c will y=(5/2)x+c touch x²/4−y²/9=1? Use those values to find the tangents.'),heading('Solution'),eq(r'c=\pm\sqrt{a^2m^2-b^2}=\pm\sqrt{4\left(\frac{25}{4}\right)-9}=\pm4,\quad a^2=4,\ b^2=9,\ m=\frac52'),paragraph('The required tangent lines are:'),eq(r'y=mx+c=\frac52x\pm4,\quad m=\frac52'),heading('(ix) Equation of a tangent line to hyperbola at a point'),paragraph('The tangent at P(x₁,y₁) to hyperbola x²/a²−y²/b²=1 has form:'),eq(r'y-y_1=m_1(x-x_1)'),note('The Remember box mistakenly uses +b² under its first radical. The correct finite-slope tangent has −b² and requires a²m²−b²>0. Equality yields an asymptote with no finite contact, not a tangent. Slopes with negative radicand give no real tangent. Vertical tangents x=±a are additional. Here c is the line intercept; it is unrelated to the previously used positive focal distance. Some pale printed reference numbers cannot be certified from the source copies.')]),
page('142_page_1.jpg',275,[paragraph('The slope m₁ at P(x₁,y₁) is found by differentiating the hyperbola with respect to x:'),eq(r'\frac{x^2}{a^2}-\frac{y^2}{b^2}=1'),eq(r'\frac{2x}{a^2}-\frac{2y}{b^2}\frac{dy}{dx}=0'),eq(r'\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{b^2x}{a^2y}'),eq(r'\left(\frac{dy}{dx}\right)_{(x_1,y_1)}=\frac{b^2x_1}{a^2y_1}=m_1','92'),paragraph('Substitute (92) into (91) for the tangent:'),eq(r'y-y_1=m_1(x-x_1)'),eq(r'y-y_1=\frac{b^2x_1}{a^2y_1}(x-x_1)'),eq(r'\frac{yy_1}{b^2}-\frac{y_1^2}{b^2}=\frac{xx_1}{a^2}-\frac{x_1^2}{a^2}\quad\Rightarrow\quad\frac{xx_1}{a^2}-\frac{yy_1}{b^2}=\frac{x_1^2}{a^2}-\frac{y_1^2}{b^2}=1'),eq(r'\frac{xx_1}{a^2}-\frac{yy_1}{b^2}=1','93'),heading('Example 26'),paragraph('Find the tangent at P(5,16/9) to hyperbola x²/9−y²/16=1.'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('Use result (93):'),eq(r'\frac{xx_1}{a^2}-\frac{yy_1}{b^2}=1,\quad a^2=9,\ b^2=16'),eq(r'\frac{x(5)}9-\frac{y(16/9)}{16}=1,\quad(x_1,y_1)=(5,16/9)'),eq(r'\frac{5x}9-\frac{16y}{144}=1'),eq(r'80x-16y=144\quad\Rightarrow\quad80x-16y-144=0\quad\Rightarrow\quad5x-y-9=0'),heading('(x) Equation of a normal line to hyperbola at a point'),paragraph('The normal at P(x₁,y₁) to x²/a²−y²/b²=1 has form:'),eq(r'y-y_1=m_2(x-x_1)','94'),paragraph('Find the normal slope by differentiating with respect to x, continued on printed 276.'),note('Example 26’s contact (5,16/9) is off the hyperbola: 25/9−16/81=209/81≠1. The displayed 5x−y−9=0 is the polar line obtained by applying the point-form formula to an off-curve point, not its tangent. Keeping x=5 and the positive branch would instead give y=16/3, with tangent 5x−3y−9=0; that is an explicitly conditional repair, not a replacement source coordinate. The point-form proof assumes P lies on the hyperbola. Its final formula covers the vertices even though the slope derivation divides by y₁.')]),
page('142_page_2.jpg',276,[eq(r'\frac{x^2}{a^2}-\frac{y^2}{b^2}=1'),eq(r'\frac{2x}{a^2}-\frac{2y}{b^2}\frac{dy}{dx}=0'),eq(r'\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{b^2x}{a^2y}'),eq(r'\left(\frac{dy}{dx}\right)_{(x_1,y_1)}=\frac{b^2x_1}{a^2y_1}=m_1'),eq(r'm_2=-\frac1{m_1}=-\frac{a^2y_1}{b^2x_1}','95'),paragraph('Substitute (95) in (94) for the normal at P:'),eq(r'y-y_1=m_2(x-x_1)'),eq(r'y-y_1=-\frac{a^2y_1}{b^2x_1}(x-x_1),\quad m_2=-\frac{a^2y_1}{b^2x_1}'),eq(r'\frac{y-y_1}{y_1/b^2}=-\frac{x-x_1}{x_1/a^2}','96'),heading('Example 27'),paragraph('Find the normal at P(5,16/9) to hyperbola x²/9−y²/16=1.'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('Use result (96):'),eq(r'-\frac{x-x_1}{x_1/a^2}=\frac{y-y_1}{y_1/b^2},\quad a^2=9,\ b^2=16'),eq(r'-\frac{x-5}{5/9}=\frac{y-16/9}{(16/9)/16}'),eq(r'-\frac{9(x-5)}5=\frac{9y-16}{1}'),eq(r'-9(x-5)(1)=5(9y-16)'),eq(r'-9x+45=45y-80\quad\Rightarrow\quad9x+45y=125'),note('Example 27 uses the same off-curve point as Example 26, so the requested normal at that point of the hyperbola is undefined. Its printed line contains the stated point but is not a normal at a curve contact. For the separately stated conditional point (5,16/3), the normal is 9x+15y=125. A division-free normal at an actual contact is (x₁/a²)(y−y₁)+(y₁/b²)(x−x₁)=0; it gives y=0 at the horizontal hyperbola’s vertices.')])]
for v in PAGES:v.update(title='CONICS-II',visualStatus='Complete source discriminant, tangent and normal algebra with separate source-error analysis',alternateVerification={'pdf':'Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf','pdfPage':(v['page']+9)//2,'side':'left' if v['page']%2 else 'right','reason':'Every radical sign, repeated coordinate and normal calculation visually checked'})
EXAMPLES=[]
for n,q,steps,ans,pg in [
('24','Find intersections of x−y−1=0 and 4x²−y²=4.',['Rewrite the line as y=x−1.','Substitute: 4x²−(x−1)²=4.','Expand: 3x²+2x−5=(x−1)(3x+5)=0.','Thus x=1 or −5/3, yielding y=0 or −8/3.','The real intersections are (1,0),(−5/3,−8/3).','Check the second: 4·25/9−64/9=36/9=4, and −5/3+8/3−1=0; the first also satisfies both equations.'],'(1,0),(−5/3,−8/3).',273),
('25','Find c so y=(5/2)x+c touches x²/4−y²/9=1, and give tangents.',['Use the slope-form tangency condition c²=a²m²−b² with a²=4,b²=9,m=5/2.','Compute c²=4·25/4−9=16, so c=±4.','Tangents: y=(5/2)x+4 and y=(5/2)x−4.','For c=4, substitution gives (9−25)x²−80x−100=0, or −4(2x+5)²=0.','Thus contact is (−5/2,−9/4). The other sign gives contact (5/2,9/4).','Both contacts satisfy x²/4−y²/9=25/16−9/16=1, and derivative b²x/(a²y)=9x/(4y)=5/2 at either contact, matching the lines.'],'c=±4; y=(5/2)x±4, contacts (∓5/2,∓9/4).',274),
('26','Find the tangent at the printed P(5,16/9) to x²/9−y²/16=1.',['Check the point before using the tangent formula.','Its curve value is 25/9 − (256/81)/16 = 25/9 − 16/81 = 209/81 ≠ 1.','Hence the source point is off the curve and no tangent at that point of the hyperbola is defined.','The printed formula gives 5x−y−9=0, which is a polar line; it does not even contain the stated point, since 25−16/9−9=128/9≠0.','If x=5 and the positive branch were intended, solve y²=16(25/9−1)=256/9, giving y=16/3. This is a conditional repair only.','At (5,16/3), the true tangent is 5x/9−y/3=1, or 5x−3y−9=0; its slope 5/3 matches the derivative there.'],'No at-point tangent for printed P(5,16/9). Conditional P(5,16/3): 5x−3y−9=0.',275),
('27','Find the normal at the printed P(5,16/9) to x²/9−y²/16=1.',['Check the curve value at the point: 25/9 − 16/81 = 209/81 ≠ 1.','Thus there is no normal at that point of the hyperbola.','The source substitutes this off-curve point in its slope formula and obtains m₂=−9(16/9)/(16·5)=−1/5.','The resulting printed line 9x+45y=125 contains (5,16/9), but membership of a line does not make the point a curve contact.','If the separately stated conditional point (5,16/3) is used, the tangent slope is 5/3 and normal slope is −3/5.','Then y−16/3=−(3/5)(x−5), or 9x+15y=125. This normal contains the repaired point and is perpendicular to its true tangent.'],'No at-point normal for printed P(5,16/9). Conditional P(5,16/3): 9x+15y=125.',276)]:
 EXAMPLES.append({'num':n,'title':'Hyperbola Intersections and Tangency','question':q,'steps':steps,'answer':ans,'progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'page':pg,'jpg':f'{(pg+9)//2:03d}_page_{1 if pg%2 else 2}.jpg','verification':'Complete source question, contact membership and radical signs checked'}]})
